          "distinct": true,
          "id": "7177c3d96fd3f8b5af508e9e0cd74391ac8a3450",
          "message": "feat(operations): fillet hollow bodies by carrying their cavity shells (#1307)\n\n## Change\n\nFollow-up to #1303 (its Codex review finding on cavity shells). On a\nbody with an enclosed cavity, every fillet that runs onto an earlier\nband was refused, even at equal radius:\n- the walking builder could not certify its reach\n(`refuse_cavity_in_blend_reach`);\n- the rolling-ball engine refuses cavity shells outright\n(`refuse_cavity_shells`);\n- the band-removal heal behind the reorder fallback refuses them too.\n\nSo the sequential-fillet and fillet-order work from #1303 never reached\nhollow parts.\n\n`fillet_cascade` gets one more fallback, `fillet_carrying_cavities`,\ntried only on a hollow body after everything else refused:\n\n1. Run the whole cascade (engines plus reorder fallback) on an\nouter-shell-only solid. A cavity shell shares no edge with the outer\nshell, so the outer shell can be filleted on its own.\n2. Accept the result only when every face the fillet **removed or\nbuilt** has a containment box clear of every cavity face's box. Those\nfaces enclose all the changed material, so a cavity clear of them is\nuntouched and still enclosed by the result.\n3. Re-attach the original cavity shells unchanged, as the walking\nbuilder already does, and add them to `face_origins.survived`.\n\nContainment boxes (`face_containment_box`): the certified distance face\nbounds where those are prunable. A cylinder patch, which the distance\nbounds deliberately leave unprunable, is boxed by its carrier's discs at\nthe wire's axial extremes. The axial coordinate is linear in the patch's\nparameters, so it peaks on the wire, and the patch never leaves its\ncarrier. Cones and anything else without a certified box keep the\noriginal refusal.\n\nScope: only fillets on the outer shell. A fillet on a cavity's own edges\nstill goes through the existing paths.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `sequential_fillets_on_a_hollow_body_carry_the_cavity`: a 20 mm box\nwith a 10 mm cavity, vertical edge then top edge at (1, 1), (1, 0.5) and\n(1, 2). Each result is fully valid and watertight. It keeps the original\ncavity shell face for face, has the plain box's topology plus exactly\n(6, 12, 8), and its volume equals the same sequence on a plain box less\n1000 mm³, within 0.01. Fails without the change.\n- `hollow_body_fillet_reaching_the_cavity_is_refused`: a cavity just\nunder the top edge, inside the material a 2 mm fillet removes, is still\nrefused and the input is unchanged. With the clearance check disabled\nthis test fails: the cavity-cutting body is accepted and passes\nvalidation. The box check is what keeps it out.\n- `cargo test -p remus-operations --lib`: 1648 passed, 7 ignored. Fillet\ntests rerun after the final lint fix: 60 passed.\n- Fillet, blend, chamfer, notch, cavity, rim, resize and face-move\nintegration tests: all 69 test binaries pass.\n- WASM fillet, sequential, mixed-notch, gridfinity and modifier tests:\n82 passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p remus-operations -p remus-wasm --all-targets -D\nwarnings` and `cargo fmt` are clean. The det-hash, wildcard-arm,\nmodule-map, doc-path, boundary and edge-domain policy checks pass.\n- Not run locally: the full workspace test and the WASM package build\nand size check. CI covers these.\n\n## Compatibility and risks\n\n- Behaviour change: fillets on hollow bodies that were refused now build\nwhen their changes stay clear of the cavities. Bodies without cavities\nand every previously successful result are unaffected, because the new\nstep runs only on a hollow body after every other attempt refused.\n- The hollow result shares its cavity shell IDs with the input, as the\nwalking builder's result already does.\n- No public API, units, tolerances, file formats or error codes change.\n\n## WASM module tier\n\nNone: no new binding. This extends the existing core `fillet` path, and\nkernel growth is small.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude\nCode](https://claude.ai/code/session_01SD4dLsGCzoXMd3hKiHc3ZE)_\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
